# The split scene: adaptive list-detail as assertions (DESIGN.md,
# Adaptive list-detail). The window asks for list_detail once; WHICH
# presentation materializes is the size class's answer, and this scene
# drives the size class with real resizes and re-asserts on the far
# side. That round trip is the whole point — a transition no code
# specializes gates nothing, which is why resize_window belongs to this
# milestone rather than preceding it.
#
# PARKED as .pending, not in tools/scenes/: check-steps requires every
# scene there to have live legs in all five runners, and only the mac
# backend has the arm so far. It moves with the fan-out (the a11y
# precedent).
#
# The exact spellings here are mac-lane literals. The SHARED scene this
# becomes will carry the bare `expect_split` — the asymmetric invariant
# that holds on every platform — because the exact class differs per
# lane while "a regular window must not show one pane while its stack
# holds two" does not.
expect_entries 0
expect_title "split"
# Wide: regular, and with nothing pushed the stack cannot be showing
# the wrong thing yet. The split arm is what renders.
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
# Push the detail. On a regular window it lands in the TRAILING pane,
# so the base root is still visible — that is what distinguishes this
# from the serial stack, where the push would cover it.
click button#0
expect_entries 1
expect label#0 "list pane"
expect label#last "detail pane"
expect_split "regular/split"
# THE CLAIM THAT MATTERS, and the one the two lines above cannot make:
# both panes are rendered AT ONCE. `expect label#N` reads kaya's model,
# and `expect_split` reads a value the arm stamped about itself — both
# pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing. These read each platform's
# REAL tree, where an element that is not on screen is not present.
expect_ax label#0 "label/list pane"
expect_ax label#last "label/detail pane"
# Narrow: the SAME scene, the same stack, one pane. Nothing in the
# guest re-declared anything — the platform re-decided.
resize_window 420x600
expect_split "compact/stacked"
expect_entries 1
# And back, because a transition that only works in one direction is
# half a feature.
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
# The stack still pops the way it always did: list-detail is a
# PRESENTATION of the stack, not a second lifecycle.
back
expect_entries 0
expect label#0 "popped detail"
